  bootwright / diff
      Compare the context's desired state with what its apply realized, object by object, file
      by file. Each side is shown as the input files that declare its objects, desired/<path>
      beside applied/<path>, or real/<path> with --observe. Without --observe it reads durable
      records only and contacts no host; with --observe it reads each realized block's target
      through that block's read-only observation. It exits 0 whenever it renders a comparison;
      with --exit-code it exits 3 instead when any object differs, is unmanaged or is unknown.
      --color selects terminal colors. --output-dir also writes both sides as trees of files,
      with notes.txt, so a directory comparison tool can show them side by side. --tool runs a
      program over each listed file pair, with the desired file first, once the result is
      written; it needs a terminal and text output and conflicts with --output-dir.

  Usage
    bootwright diff [flags]

  Command flags
      --exit-code   Exit 3 when any object differs, is unmanaged or is unknown
      --observe     Read each realized block's target through its read-only observation
      --output-dir  Write both sides as files into a directory that does not exist or is empty
      --tool        Show each listed file pair with a comparison program

  Common flags
      --ascii       Use ASCII status markers
      --color       Select terminal colors (values: auto, always, never) · default auto
      --context     Select a context (default: current context)
      --detail      Select human detail (values: compact, full) · default compact
      -h, --help    Show help for this command
      -o, --output  Select output mode (values: text, json, yaml) · default text
      --progress    Select progress animation (values: auto, always, never) · default auto
      --width       Set presentation width (0: automatic) · default 0

  Examples
    bootwright diff --context lab --observe
